  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Aerospace recorded $1.1B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Aerospace won 160 awards so far, with an average award size of $6.8M. Aerospace obligations fell down 22.5% year over year. Aerospace obligations by fiscal year show FY21 at $1.2B, FY22 at $1.2B, FY23 at $1.3B, FY24 at $1.3B, FY25 at $1.4B, and FY26 at $1.1B. Aerospace sells a large share of its work in research and development, where Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences (except Nanotechnology and Biotechnology) accounts for $1.1B. Aerospace receives most federal buying from the Department of Defense, which obligated $940M in FY2026 to date. Aerospace also received obligations from the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $99.5M and the Department of Energy at $31.9M. Smaller federal buyers include the Department of Commerce at $13.2M, the Department of the Interior at $3.3M, and the Department of Transportation at $1.7M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are Aerospace obligations across agencies?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $940M in FY2026 to date. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration follows at $99.5M, and the Department of Energy follows at $31.9M. The Department of Commerce, Department of the Interior, and Department of Transportation obligated $13.2M, $3.3M, and $1.7M respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What program areas drive Aerospace obligations?",
        "body": "Research and development in the physical and engineering sciences is the largest program area, accounting for $1.1B. Aerospace also competes across other NAICS categories reported on the page, while award volume totals 160 and the average award size is $6.8M."
      }
    ]
  },
